nventional unrolling methods usually need more memory consumption as all the intermediate computational results have to be recorded. However, when updating (7) continuously, Alt-Diff does not need to save the results of the previous round. Instead, it only saves the results of the last round, that is, the previous \u2202xk\u2202\u03b8 is replaced by the \u2202xk+1 \u2202\u03b8 iteratively.\nConvergence analysis Lemma 4.1. (Fixed point iteration Kirk (1965)) Suppose G(x\u0303, \u03b8) is an L-Lipschitz continuous function, for any x\u03030 \u2208 X , by the iteration x\u0303k+1 = G(x\u0303k, \u03b8) converges to a fixed point x\u0303\u22c6(\u03b8).\nThe linear system (7) can be viewed as an operator G(x\u0303, \u03b8), with x\u0303 collecting the variables [x, s, \u03bb, \u03bd]. By Lemma 4.1, we can derive the following theorem.\nTheorem 4.2. Suppose the gradient of the solution x\u22c6 with respect to the parameters \u03b8 obtained by CvxpyLayer Agrawal et al. (2019a) through differentiating KKT conditions implicitly is \u2202x \u22c6\n\u2202\u03b8 . Let \u2202xk \u2202\u03b8 denote the gradient obtained by the k-th primal differentiation (7a). Then\nlim k\u2192\u221e \u2202xk \u2202\u03b8 = \u2202x\u22c6 \u2202\u03b8 . (8)\nThis theorem shows that the gradient obtained by Alt-Diff is consistent with the one obtained by the gradient of the optimality conditions implicitly. The detailed proof as well as the procedure of differentiating KKT conditions is presented in the Appendix C."
